q=A.a(["Best & Average Case: O(1) Time for Insertion, Deletion, and Searching (Contains)","Worst Case: O(n) Time (Triggered by severe Hash Collisions)","Space Complexity: O(n) (Requires underlying bucket array and node structures)"],m)
k=A.c_(A.a2(["Python",'# Python natively supports Sets\ndef hash_set_demo():\n    my_set = set()\n    \n    # Insertion - O(1)\n    my_set.add(10)\n    my_set.add(20)\n    my_set.add(10) # Ignored! 10 is already in the set\n    \n    # Searching - O(1)\n    if 20 in my_set:\n        print("20 exists!")\n        \n    # Deletion - O(1)\n    my_set.remove(10)\n    \n    # Length is currently 1 (only 20 remains)\n    print(len(my_set))\n',"Java",'import java.util.HashSet;\n\nclass HashSetDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();\n        \n        // Insertion - O(1)\n        set.add(10);\n        set.add(20);\n        set.add(10); // Ignored! Automatically handles duplicates\n        \n        // Searching - O(1)\n        if (set.contains(20)) {\n            System.out.println("20 exists!");\n        }\n        \n        // Deletion - O(1)\n        set.remove(10);\n        \n        // Size is 1\n        System.out.println(set.size());\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\n#include <unordered_set> // Unordered Set uses Hashing\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid hashSetDemo() {\n    unordered_set<int> mySet;\n    \n    // Insertion - O(1)\n    mySet.insert(10);\n    mySet.insert(20);\n    mySet.insert(10); // Ignored!\n    \n    // Searching - O(1)\n    if (mySet.find(20) != mySet.end()) {\n        cout << "20 exists!" << endl;\n    }\n    \n    // Deletion - O(1)\n    mySet.erase(10);\n    \n    // Size is 1\n    cout << mySet.size() << endl;\n}\n',"JavaScript",'function hashSetDemo() {\n    // JS natively supports the Set object\n    const mySet = new Set();\n    \n    // Insertion - O(1)\n    mySet.add(10);\n    mySet.add(20);\n    mySet.add(10); // Ignored!\n    \n    // Searching - O(1)\n    if (mySet.has(20)) {\n        console.log("20 exists!");\n    }\n    \n    // Deletion - O(1)\n    mySet.delete(10);\n    \n    // Size is 1\n    console.log(mySet.size);\n}\n'],i,i),q,j,"Need to remove duplicates from a massive dataset instantly? Welcome to the Hash Set! Built directly on top of Hash Table architecture, a Hash Set strips away the 'Values' and focuses entirely on the 'Keys'. Learn how this mathematically inspired data structure acts as the ultimate gatekeeper, granting instant O(1) lookups and guaranteeing absolute uniqueness across your data.","hash-sets",n,"Hash Sets",A.a(["Mutating Objects: If you store a complex object in a Hash Set and later modify its properties, its hash code changes. The Set will permanently lose track of it, creating a memory leak.","Assuming Order: Iterating over a Hash Set will return elements in a seemingly random, chaotic order. Never assume 'first in, first out'.","Confusing Sets with Maps: A Hash Map stores Key-Value pairs (e.g., 'Alice' -> 95). A Hash Set only stores single Keys for existence checking (e.g., 'Is Alice in the system?')."],m),k,A.a([A.Y(2,"A Hash Set is quite literally just a Hash Table/Map that ignores the 'Value' column. It uses the exact same hash functions and buckets to store your data, granting it the same O(1) performance.",A.a(["As a Binary Search Tree.","As a standard Array that gets sorted every time a new element is added.","As a Hash Table where the keys are the elements you want to store, and the values are just empty dummy placeholders.","As a doubly linked list."],m),"Under the hood, how is a Hash Set typically implemented in most modern programming languages?"),A.Y(1,"A Hash Set enforces absolute uniqueness. The first '5' is added, the '10' is added, but the second '5' evaluates to the exact same hash code and key as the first one, so it is silently ignored/overwritten. The size remains 2.",A.a(["3","2","1","It will throw a Runtime Error."],m),"If you run the code: set.add(5); set.add(10); set.add(5); What is the final size of the Hash Set?"),A.Y(1,"Hash Sets do not have indexes. Data is placed strictly based on its mathematical hash code. What you inserted 1st might land in bucket 8, and what you inserted 2nd might land in bucket 2.",A.a(["Because Hash Sets are read-only after creation.","Because elements in a Hash Set are scrambled by the hash function and stored in random buckets, completely destroying the concept of insertion order or indexes.","Because the elements are stored in reverse order.","Because the modulo operator deletes the indexes."],m),"Why is a Hash Set completely incapable of returning the '3rd element' you inserted?"),A.Y(2,"Hash Sets are the ultimate tool for finding unique elements. By dumping all 1 million IP addresses into a Hash Set, all the duplicates are instantly destroyed, leaving you with the exact count of unique visitors in O(n) time.",A.a(["Finding the player with the lowest score.","Maintaining a playlist of songs in the exact order the user queued them.","Taking a list of 1 million website visitors and finding out exactly how many UNIQUE IP addresses visited the site.","Storing user passwords alongside their usernames."],m),"Which of the following problems is a Hash Set perfectly designed to solve?")],h),"assets/mds/dsa/hash/hashsets.md",B.S0)
